steps.body:
A shop shows dozens of product cards that look alike and hold different products. You don't want to write the card's markup dozens of times, and when the design changes, you want to change it in one place. Writing the markup once and handing each card its data is what components are for, and the data you hand over is called **props**.

## TypeScript, so you can read the code

Most React teams write **TypeScript**, and so does this course, so first, what you need to read it. TypeScript is JavaScript with **types** written in: notes that say what kind of value each thing holds. Nothing in plain JavaScript stops you passing a product's name where a whole product belongs, and you'd find out in the browser, maybe from a customer. With types, your editor underlines the mistake as you type, and `tsc`, the TypeScript compiler, checks a whole project at once. The types are stripped out before the code runs, so the browser still gets plain JavaScript. TypeScript files end in `.ts`, or `.tsx` when they hold JSX, and the next lesson sets up both tools.

Here's the type for the shop's products, from `src/lib/products.ts`:

```ts
export type Product = {
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  priceCents: number;
  inStock: boolean;
};
```

- `type Product = { ... }` gives a name to a shape of object: which fields it has, and what each one holds.
- `string` is text, `number` is a number, and `boolean` is `true` or `false`.
- `Product[]` means an array of products, and you'll see it in the first example.

The `slug` is a product's short name in addresses, like `trail-pack-45`. Prices are whole cents, `18900` for $189.00, because adding up dollars with decimals in JavaScript can come out a cent off.

## A component with props

A **component** is a function that returns JSX, as the Why React Exists lesson put it: it takes data and returns what that piece of the screen should look like. Here's the card, in its own file, `src/components/ProductCard.tsx`:

```tsx
import { formatPrice } from "../lib/format";
import type { Product } from "../lib/products";

type ProductCardProps = {
  product: Product;
  size?: "sm" | "md";
};

export function ProductCard({ product, size = "md" }: ProductCardProps) {
  return (
    <article className={`card card-${size}`}>
      <h3>{product.name}</h3>
      <p>{formatPrice(product.priceCents)}</p>
    </article>
  );
}
```

`import` and `export` are the modules you met in the web course, often called ES modules, after ECMAScript, JavaScript's official name. `import type` brings in a type alone, which disappears with the other types. The props type has two new pieces. The `?` in `size?` makes a prop optional: you can leave it out, and then it's `undefined`. `"sm" | "md"` is a **union**: the value must be one of those exact strings, and `|` reads as or. `: ProductCardProps` after the parameter says the props have that shape. The class name is a template literal, the backtick string that fills in each `${...}`.

## Using it

You use a component like a tag: `<ProductCard product={pack} size="sm" />`. Each attribute becomes a **prop**, short for property. React gathers them into one object and passes it to your function as its only argument, so the card gets `{ product: pack, size: "sm" }`. The `{ product, size = "md" }` in its parameter list is **destructuring**: it pulls those fields out of the object into variables with the same names. The `= "md"` is a **default**, used when the prop is left out.

The capital letter is a rule, not a habit. Here's what the compiler made of a lowercase tag and a capitalized one:

```javascript
export const a = /* @__PURE__ */ _jsx("article", {
  className: "card",
  children: name
});
export const b = /* @__PURE__ */ _jsx(ProductCard, {
  product: pack,
  size: "sm"
});
```

A lowercase tag becomes a string, the name of an HTML element. A capitalized one becomes your function itself, and when React meets an element whose type is a function, it calls the function and uses what comes back. Write `<productCard />` and React makes an unknown HTML tag called productcard instead, and no card appears.

## Props are read-only

A component never changes its props. `product.name = "Sale!"` inside the card would change the parent's data behind its back, and the page and the data would drift apart. Data flows one way, down from parent to child, as the Why React Exists lesson showed. To show something new, the parent passes new props.

## Components must be pure

Working out what a component shows means calling it, and React calls it whenever it needs to, as often as it likes. While you're developing, it even calls each component twice on purpose, which the next lesson explains. So a component must be **pure**: the same props always give the same JSX, and while it runs it changes nothing outside itself. No changing variables declared outside, no editing the DOM, no requests to a server. Here's one that breaks the rule:

```tsx
let issued = 0;

export function Ticket() {
  issued = issued + 1;
  return <p>Ticket number {issued}</p>;
}
```

Two tickets on a page should say 1 and 2. In development they said Ticket number 2 and Ticket number 4, since each was called twice. Code that changes things belongs in event handlers, which run when someone clicks or types, and the State and Events lesson starts there.

