steps.body:
Many components are frames around other content: a card with a border and a heading, a page with a header and a footer, a dialog box. A frame shouldn't need to know what goes inside it, so that the same card can hold a product, a review, or a login form. React lets you pass JSX into a component, the same way you put content inside an HTML tag.

## The children prop

Whatever you put between a component's opening and closing tags arrives as a prop called `children`. You met it in the first part, where the compiler put the text of an `h1` in a `children` field. It works the same for your own components. Here's a card frame, in `src/components/Card.tsx`:

```tsx
import type { ReactNode } from "react";

type CardProps = {
  title: string;
  children: ReactNode;
};

export function Card({ title, children }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <section className="card">
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      {children}
    </section>
  );
}
```

`Card` draws the frame and the heading, and puts `children` wherever the content goes. It doesn't know or care what the content is.

`ReactNode` is a type React exports for anything it can show: an element, a string, a number, an array of those, or `null`, `undefined`, `true`, or `false`, which show nothing. It's the usual type for any prop that takes content.

## Layout components

A **layout component** arranges other components and leaves the content to whoever uses it. A page layout with a header, a main area, and a footer is the classic one: every page in the shop uses it, so changing the header changes it on every page at once. The second example builds one.

## Slots: JSX in any prop

`children` is one place to put content, and some frames need several. Since JSX makes an element, which is an ordinary value, any prop can hold JSX, and props used that way are often called **slots**:

```tsx
<PageLayout header={<SiteHeader />} sidebar={<CategoryFilters />}>
  <ProductGrid products={products} />
</PageLayout>
```

The layout decides where the header, the sidebar, and the page's own content go. Each page decides what they are.

## Composition, not inheritance

Some languages build a new part from an old one by **inheritance**: you declare that a new kind of thing extends an old one, then change a few of its pieces. Old React code had **class components**, written as `class ProductCard extends React.Component`, and even then, React's advice was never to build one component on top of another that way. Today components are functions, and you build bigger ones by putting smaller ones together, which is called **composition**. A sale card doesn't extend `Card`. It renders a `Card` with a sale badge and the product inside. Each piece stays small, you can read each one on its own, and you can swap one without touching the others.

Composition also solves a problem you'll meet as an app grows. Sometimes a value has to pass through several components that don't use it, only to reach one deep inside that does, which is called **prop drilling**. Passing the finished JSX as children often removes the middle steps, since the outer component builds the inner piece itself and hands it straight to where it goes. The Sharing State lesson comes back to this, with tools for the cases composition can't solve.

